  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 212 AM CST Sat Nov 14 2026\n\nA slow moving large scale upper low over the northeast states will \ngradually migrate eastward over the weekend, becoming less of an \ninfluence on central IL, as low level northerly flow acquires more \nof a westerly component and deflects lake effect cloud cover \neastward and away from east-central IL. Meanwhile, an elongated high \npressure center from Missouri through the upper Midwest into Canada \nwill build and shift southeastward, parking over central IL by late \nin the weekend, for very light winds and mostly clear skies. \nAlthough the high will continue to shift eastward toward the mid-\nAtlantic coast through the week, it looks to keep any precipitation \naway from central IL through at least Wednesday, if not Thursday and \nFriday as well as it looks to deflect the next system well into the \nupper Midwest and potentially prevent a late week cold front from \nreaching central IL. Nevertheless, a slight chance for precipitation \ncan't be ruled out with this feature as far east as the I-55 \ncorridor by Friday.\n\nTemperatures will remain below normal today and Sunday as the cool \nair mass lingers over the area and slowly moderates. Nighttime \ntemperatures will show less tendency for any upward trends as the \nclear skies and light winds promote good radiational cooling. Expect \nhighs in the upper 40s to lower 50s today, lower to mid 50s Sunday, \nand generally mid 50s Monday. Lows will be mainly mid 20s tonight \nand Sunday night, then upper 20s to lower 30s Monday night and \nTuesday night. As southerly flow develops midweek, highs should \nreach the mid to upper 50s Tuesday, around 60 Wednesday, and lower \nto mid 60s Thursday. The warming trend could continue into Friday if \nthe cold front is unable to push through IL.",
